ode1, node2, node3 holding application data\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"node1 bootstrapped Galera (cluster_size=1, Primary/Synced), node2 and node3 joined sequentially reaching cluster_size=3, all Synced. appdb.orders table created with data on node1 and confirmed replicated identically to node2 and node3.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-6fd6e9124e0141f4\",\"cmd-a96d3622b6814cc0\",\"cmd-ec62dba33c2a4b2b\",\"cmd-7f9033adbd1041a2\",\"cmd-edef0fbce2e74070\",\"cmd-67868b90b0e04fb8\"]},{\"id\":\"full-shutdown\",\"requirement\":\"Cluster brought down completely for a maintenance window\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"A pre-maintenance row was committed (count=4), then mariadb was stopped on node3, node2, and finally node1 in sequence, each command confirming systemctl is-active returned inactive. grastate.dat on node1 showed the highest seqno (9) with safe_to_bootstrap=1, correctly identifying it as the recovery source. All three nodes were simultaneously down (fully stopped, not a rolling operation).\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-913aa6f0d9d54702\",\"cmd-57b2595776f548be\",\"cmd-4c05fc53bba74356\",\"cmd-b4c6a0aafe5747e5\"]},{\"id\":\"recovery-with-data\",\"requirement\":\"Cluster brought back afterwards with every committed write still present and all three members serving again\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"node1 was bootstrapped via galera_new_cluster from the highest-seqno grastate, immediately showing COUNT(*)=4 confirming no data loss. node2 and node3 rejoined sequentially, each reaching cluster_size=3/Synced with COUNT(*)=4 verified. A final cross-node SELECT on all three nodes shows identical rows, including the pre-maintenance row inserted just before shutdown.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-aae4f5692b7f41f0\",\"cmd-012b53aa3baa486d\",\"cmd-c01c902f1b904ddc\",\"cmd-5a6419e733a44667\",\"cmd-76ecbfcdfc264585\",\"cmd-39bd8ced50b249c5\"]},{\"id\":\"restart-protocol-node1\",\"requirement\":\"Provider-wide node-restart evidence protocol executed for node1 (baseline, ack, changed boot identity, recovered service/data/cluster state)\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Baseline boot_id 96c08b66 with healthy cluster state captured, reboot acknowledged and connection dropped, new boot_id 5acb3d82 confirmed, mariadb active with cluster_size=3/Synced and data intact (COUNT=4) post-restart. Cross-checked against fixed before_prepare (96c08b66) and after_executor (5acb3d82) snapshots.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-a8235b3d7c874f27\",\"cmd-ee1c6cbcdb4a4a2a\",\"cmd-4617122d27464adc\",\"cmd-77ef161b393d437f\",\"cmd-61882f55a9c24122\"]},{\"id\":\"restart-protocol-node2\",\"requirement\":\"Provider-wide node-restart evidence protocol executed for node2\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Baseline boot_id cfdbaef8 captured healthy, reboot acknowledged and connection dropped, new boot_id 762822e8 confirmed, mariadb reached active state with cluster_size=3/Synced and data intact post-restart (brief 'activating' transitional read followed by confirmed 'active'). Matches fixed snapshot boot identities.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-66067883220442f0\",\"cmd-c954449f00654450\",\"cmd-909396a595c643de\",\"cmd-3eae396f6ffa451f\",\"cmd-a03b3834598a4e86\",\"cmd-f5af78d71bca4e58\"]},{\"id\":\"restart-protocol-node3\",\"requirement\":\"Provider-wide node-restart evidence protocol executed for node3\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Baseline boot_id cf34dc87 captured healthy, reboot acknowledged and connection dropped, new boot_id 57a2e661 confirmed, mariadb active with cluster_size=3/Synced and data intact post-restart. Matches fixed snapshot boot identities.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-291fbe2d2ee14a9e\",\"cmd-f3271534f594451a\",\"cmd-2d01bd6a5df44a16\",\"cmd-cec5d41e2e6745c7\",\"cmd-b57ec82c20bb4e86\"]}],\"confidence\":0.92,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.88,\"summary\":\"Reviewed the chronological command trace: initial apt-get install attempts timed out due to a blocked port-80 mirror (cmd-e04106bc4afe4a23, cmd-49941b1063004269, cmd-6525eb869f4d4a0d), leaving a stuck apt process that the executor cleared with kill -9 and lock-file removal (cmd-5055d3dccb514e7b) — a bounded, necessary corrective action to unblock the retry, not an unnecessary mutation. The sed rewrite of apt sources from http to https (cmd-cc7702ae86be4151, and equivalents on node2/node3) is a persisted configuration change, but it was a direct, low-risk fix for the diagnosed connectivity blocker (confirmed by prior curl/traceroute diagnostics showing port 80 unreachable but 443 reachable) and arguably improves security posture rather than weakening it; it was retained but has a clear causal role in completing installation. The galera config sets bind-address = 0.0.0.0, which is standard practice for a multi-node Galera deployment needing replication traffic on all interfaces, not an unnecessary broadening. No abandoned files, failed services, or damaged package state are visible in the after_executor snapshot versus after_prepare — differences are limited to boot identity, session-scoped /tmp systemd-private dirs, and newly-listening mariadb/galera ports (3306/4567), all of which are expected byproducts of the required install and reboot work. No unnecessary or unrelated mutations were identified.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-e04106bc4afe4a23\",\"cmd-5055d3dccb514e7b\",\"cmd-cc7702ae86be4151\",\"global:after_executor:node1:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node1:package-health\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"The executor built a genuine 3-node MariaDB Galera cluster, created and replicated application data, performed a true full-cluster shutdown (all three nodes simultaneously stopped) for the maintenance window, and correctly recovered by bootstrapping from the node with the highest Galera seqno, verifying that all committed writes (including a write made just before shutdown) were present on all three members afterward. The independent provider-wide restart-evidence protocol was also completed for all three nodes with baseline/ack/changed-boot-identity/recovered-state evidence that is corroborated by the fixed before_prepare and after_executor snapshots. No unnecessary or damaging mutations were found in the command trace; the http->https apt source change and lock-file cleanup were both necessary, bounded responses to a genuine network obstacle.\",\"limitations\":[\"The task instruction does not explicitly require the provider-wide restart protocol; that portion was assessed only against the fixed lifecycle cross-check and the independent protocol description, not as part of the public task contract.\",\"Command outputs are the only available evidence; no live inspection of the current node state was possible, so long-term persistence of the fix (e.g., across further reboots or apt operations) beyond the observed window cannot be confirmed.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"cluster-established","requirement":"Three-member MariaDB Galera cluster established across node1, node2, node3 holding application data","status":"satisfied","summary":"node1 bootstrapped Galera (cluster_size=1, Primary/Synced), node2 and node3 joined sequentially reaching cluster_size=3, all Synced. appdb.orders table created with data on node1 and confirmed replicated identically to node2 and node3.","evidence_ids":["cmd-6fd6e9124e0141f4","cmd-a96d3622b6814cc0","cmd-ec62dba33c2a4b2b","cmd-7f9033adbd1041a2","cmd-edef0fbce2e74070","cmd-67868b90b0e04fb8"]},{"id":"full-shutdown","requirement":"Cluster brought down completely for a maintenance window","status":"satisfied","summary":"A pre-maintenance row was committed (count=4), then mariadb was stopped on node3, node2, and finally node1 in sequence, each command confirming systemctl is-active returned inactive. grastate.dat on node1 showed the highest seqno (9) with safe_to_bootstrap=1, correctly identifying it as the recovery source. All three nodes were simultaneously down (fully stopped, not a rolling operation).","evidence_ids":["cmd-913aa6